[{"page":1,"text":"NOTICE OF A REGULAR\n\nPLANNING COMMISSION MEETING\n\nMay 6, 2026, at 7:00 PM\n________________\nPUBLIC NOTICE is hereby given that the Vineyard Planning Commission will hold a regularly\nscheduled Planning Commission meeting on Wednesday, May 6, 2026, at 7:00 PM, in the City\nCouncil Chambers at City Hall, 125 South Main Street, Vineyard, UT. This meeting can also be\nviewed on our live stream page.\nPlanning Commissioners Present: Daria Evans, Brad Fagg, Martina Huntington, Graden\n\nOstler, David Pearce, and Nathan Steele.\nStaff Present: Planner Anthony Fletcher, City Recorder Robin Bond, Public Works Director\n\nNaseem Ghandour, Assistant Engineer Patrick James.\nAlso Present: Jane Pearce, Russell Evans, Karen Cornelious, Bronson Tatton, BJ Laterveer,\nand others who did not sign in.\n\n1.\n\nCALL TO ORDER/INVOCATION/INSPIRATIONAL THOUGHT/PLEDGE OF\n\nALLEGIANCE\nChairperson Steele called the meeting to order at 7 pm. Commissioner Evans gave the\npledge of allegiance and invocation.\n\n2.\n\nPRESENTATIONS/RECOGNITIONS/AWARDS/PROCLAMATIONS\n\nNone\n\n3.\n\nPUBLIC COMMENTS PC\nTime dedicated for public comment. Comments will be limited to three (3) minutes. No\nactions may be taken by the Planning Commission due to the need for proper public\nnoticing. Public comments can be submitted ahead of time to planning@vineyardutah.gov.\n\nNone.\n\n4.\n\nCONSENT ITEMS\n\nNone.\n\n5.\n\nBUSINESS ITEMS\n\n5.1. Chair and Vice-Chair Re-election\n\nThe Commission will nominate and then elect the standing Chair and Vice-Chair\npositions for the 2026 calendar year.\n\nPage 1 of 5; May 6, 2026, Planning Commission Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"The Chair called for nominations.\n\nNOMINATION: Commissioner David Pierce nominated Commissioner Nathan\n\nSteele. Commissioner Brad Fagg seconded the motion. Yes: Commissioners Evans,\n\nFagg, Huntington, Ostler, Pearce, and Steele. No: None.\n\nThe Chair called for nominations for Vice Chair.\n\nNOMINATION: Commissioner Graden Ostler nominated Commissioner David\n\nPearce. Commissioner Martina Huntington seconded the motion. Yes: Commissioners\n\nEvans, Fagg, Huntington, Ostler, Pearce, and Steele. No: None.\n\nCommissioner Nathan Steele would serve as Chair, and Commissioner David\n\nPearce would serve as Vice Chair.\n\n5.2. Conditional Use and Site Plan Application- Tire Auto Body Shop and Retail at\n\n430 S Geneva Road, Regional Commercial Zone\n\nGuilford Rand requests approval of a site plan for a two-building commercial\ndevelopment at 430 S Geneva Road in the Regional Commercial Zone, including a\ntire/auto body shop, general retail space, restaurant, parking, landscaping, and related\nsite improvements.\n\nThe Planning Commission reviewed a request from Guilford Rand for approval of a\nsite plan and conditional use permit for a two-building commercial development at\n\n430 S Geneva Road in the Regional Commercial Zone, including a tire/auto body\nshop, retail space, restaurant use, parking, landscaping, and related improvements.\nPlanner Fletcher presented the project and noted that the applicant added additional\nparking as requested. Commissioners discussed access at 400 South, roadway\nalignment, potential corner barriers, signage, and the design of the two-story office\nspace. Concerns were raised regarding the use of honey locust trees, with both\ncommissioners and a resident, Russell Evan, noting they are messy; the Commission\nrecommended consultation with the City Arborist on tree selection. The City Engineer\nstated that two site entrances are required for safety and that a crosswalk or signal will\nbe installed in the area. Chairman Steele confirmed that the City is not responsible for\ntree maintenance and requested that a maintenance and cleanliness clause be included\nto ensure the applicant maintains the parking lot and landscaping.\n\nMOTION: Commissioner Pierce motioned that the conditional use request is\napproved with the conditions that we’ve stated in the recommendations.\n\nCommissioner Evans seconded the motion. Yes: Commissioners Evans, Fagg,\n\nHuntington, Ostler, Pearce, and Steele. No: None. Motion Passed 6-0.\n\nSite Plan Amendment: Utah City Wellness Center and Resort Pool Phase 2\n\n5.3.\n\nPlanning Commission – Meeting Minutes Summary\n\nUtah City Wellness Center and Resort Pool, Phase 2 Site Plan Amendment\n\nPage 2 of 5; May 6, 2026, Planning Commission Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"The Planning Commission reviewed a request from Flagborough for approval of a site\nplan amendment for the Utah City Wellness Center and Resort Pool Phase 2. The\namendment was limited to updates to building elevations and landscaping, with no\nother changes to the previously approved site plan.\nPlanner Fletcher presented the proposed revisions, outlining updates to the building\nelevations and landscape plan. He confirmed that the applicant has agreed to meet all\npedestrian-friendly landscaping requirements. Construction-related parking and safety\nconcerns were discussed.\n\nRepresentatives Bronson Tatton and BJ Laterveer, on behalf of Utah City\n(Flagborough), addressed questions from the Commission. They explained that the\nexterior elevation changes to the changing rooms and concessions were intended to\nbetter align with the surrounding architectural styles and to ensure an authentic\nappearance.\nCommissioner Pearce clarified that the pool is anticipated to open for public use in\n\n2027. Commissioner Evans asked about the removal of demolition materials; Mr.\nTatton stated that excavated soil was relocated for fill needs in other areas of Utah\n\nCity. Commissioner Evans also requested consideration of glazing or decorative\ntreatment on a wall, and Mr. Laterveer reiterated the intent for the building to be\nvisually authentic. Commissioner Evan suggested Monet’s Water Lilies be included in\nthe artwork. Both Mr. Tatton and Mr. Laterveer agreed that it was a great idea.\n\nCommissioner Marina inquired about ADA accessibility during construction. Mr.\n\nTatton confirmed that ADA access would remain available throughout construction\nand that the number of ADA parking stalls would meet State code requirements.\n\nCommissioner Graden asked about ownership and public access; Mr. Tatton stated\nthat the facility will operate as a not-for-profit, will be open for public enjoyment, and\nthat the City will retain ownership of the land.\n\nPublic Comment:\n•\n•\n•\n\nKaren Corneilous, resident, expressed concerns regarding parking, particularly\nrelated to parking availability at the All-Abilities Park.\n\nJane Pearce of Cascade asked about zero-scaping and water usage.\n\nTerry Ewing of Villas inquired about the length of the pool’s seasonal\noperation, swim meets, and swim lessons.\n\nThe Chair reminded attendees that the scope of the agenda item was limited to\nbuilding elevations and landscaping.\nIn response to public comments, Mr. Tatton stated that waterwise landscaping would\nbe implemented. He explained that the upper pool would be open year-round with\n\nPage 3 of 5; May 6, 2026, Planning Commission Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"heated decks, while other pools would be closed during the winter months. Mr. Tatton\nconcluded by thanking Utah City for donating funds to support the project.\n\nMOTION: Commissioner Steele motioned to accept the site plan amendment for\n\nUtah City Wellness Center and Resort Pool phase 2, with the conditions as\npresented, with the suggested modification for the last one in the Spa building.\n\nCommissioner Pearce seconded the motion. Yes: Commissioners Evans, Fagg,\n\nHuntington, Ostler, Pearce, and Steele. No: None. Motion Passed 6-0.\n\n6.\n\nWORK SESSION\n\n6.4. Floodplain Regulation Update, National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP)\n\nCompliance\n\nUpdates to the City’s floodplain management ordinance are required to maintain\ncompliance with the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) following updated\n\nFEMA flood hazard determinations.\n\nPlanning Commission – Meeting Minutes Summary\n\nFloodplain Regulation Update – National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) Compliance\n\nThe Planning Commission reviewed a proposed update to the City’s floodplain management\nordinance, which is required to maintain compliance with the National Flood Insurance Program\n(NFIP) following updated FEMA (Federal Emergency Management Agency) flood hazard\ndeterminations.\nPublic Works Director and City Engineer Naseem Ghandour explained that the update is part of\na public input process requested by the State. He stated that the purpose of the ordinance update\nis to reduce flood risk, protect public safety, maintain the City’s eligibility for flood insurance,\nand preserve access to federal disaster assistance in the event of a flood-related emergency.\nChairperson Steele confirmed that the Planning Commission’s role at this stage was to provide\ninput on the proposed ordinance. Mr. Ghandour confirmed that an ordinance would be presented\nto the City Council in June. He noted that the ordinance is based on a State-provided model and\nis intended to ensure compliance with FEMA and the NFIP, while aligning with City and State\nof Utah requirements.\nMr. Ghandour informed the Commission that a public comment period would be held from May\n1 through May 31, 2026. Public comments may be submitted through the City’s website, and\nnotice of the comment period would be provided through public meetings, social media, and the\n\nCity’s website.\nCommissioner Martina asked about public awareness and education related to emergency\nprocedures. Mr. Ghandour stated that public education was not addressed within the scope of\nthis ordinance; however, he noted that the Utah Department of Public Safety offers resources for\n\nPage 4 of 5; May 6, 2026, Planning Commission Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"public access. He encouraged commissioners and members of the public to contact his office\nwith any questions or concerns regarding the floodplain regulations.\n\nLinks for information and comments: . Public Notices\nhttps://www.vineyardutah.gov/government/public_notices.php\n\nFloodplain Ordinance Public Comments\n\n7.\n\nSTAFF AND COMMISSION REPORTS\n\nNone.\n\n8.\n\nADJOURNMENT\n\nThe meeting adjourned at 8:50 pm.\n\nMINUTES WERE APPROVED ON:\n\nCERTIFIED CORRECT BY:\n\nPage 5 of 5; May 6, 2026, Planning Commission Agenda","ocr":false}]